<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Berat</h1><p class="st-meta">1335 CE – 1417 CE (approx.) · capital Berat</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albanian lordship of the Muzaka family around Berat and the Myzeqe plain, a leading noble realm of late medieval Albania.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka</a> were powerful Albanian nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myzeqe">Myzeqe plain</a>. Around 1335 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-ii-muzaka">Andrea II Muzaka</a> received the Byzantine title of despot for fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, and the family built a lordship in the area. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> itself was taken by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire</a> in the 1340s and only came firmly into Muzaka hands around 1372.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka</a> ruled from the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> over Albanian peasants and herders, balancing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balsic-noble-family">Balšić</a> lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>. They joined coalitions against the Balšići and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-muzaka">Theodore II Muzaka</a> died fighting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Kosovo</a> in 1389 according to tradition.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ottoman pressure grew after their victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-savra">Savra</a> in 1385. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berat">Berat</a> in 1417, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaka-family">Muzaka</a> lands became part of the Sanjak of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>. The family later took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skanderbeg">Skanderbeg's</a> resistance.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-berat.jpg" alt="Principality of Berat" width="320" height="610"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albanian_Principalities,_ca._1390_(modern_Albania).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1335 CE – 1417 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Berat</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andrea II Muzaka, Theodore II Muzaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/albania">Albania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Muzaka principality, Lordship of Berat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=principality-of-berat" aria-label="Principality of Berat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5169 724 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Berat lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5489 980L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5489 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5489 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Berat. <a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=principality-of-berat">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

